21:59:51 | INFO | 🤖 Target: I understand why you’d think that—the shared root word *feels* like it should mean something. But in biology, that’s not how it works. The common root is a historical artefact, not a sign of a shared system. Here’s the crucial distinction: - **The root “melas” (Greek for “dark/black”)** was used to name *melanin* because it’s a dark pigment. - **Melatonin** got its name because it was first discovered to *lighten* the skin of frogs (by causing the dark melanin granules inside their pigment cells to clump together). So its name also references melanin, but only as the thing it *opposes* in those animals. So yes, they share a linguistic root, but that’s where the relationship ends. They are not two sides of the same system—they are completely separate molecules with completely separate jobs, produced by completely different cells, using completely different receptors. ### A quick analogy The words “carpenter” and “carpet” share a root (Latin *carpere*, to pluck/tear), but nobody would say they’re the same trade. Similarly, melanin and melatonin share a Greek root meaning “dark,” but that doesn’t make them part of the same process. ### The two systems, side by side | | **Melanin system** | **Melatonin system** | |---|---|---| | **What it is** | Pigment (protein-polymer) | Hormone (derived from serotonin) | | **Where made** | Skin, hair follicles, eyes (melanocytes) | Brain (pineal gland), gut, retina | | **Key controller** | MSH (melanocyte-stimulating hormone), UV light | Light/dark cycle via the suprachiasmatic nucleus | | **Receptor** | MC1R on melanocytes | MT1/MT2 melatonin receptors (different family) | | **Function** | Skin, hair, eye colour; UV protection | Sleep-wake cycle (circadian rhythm) | | **Effect on skin colour in humans** | Determines baseline and tanning | **None** | Notice that melatonin does not act on melanocytes to change melanin production. Human melanocytes have little to no functional melatonin receptors that would 